# What it is for. A foreign P4 pipeline carries no NDTwin LLDP, so on a foreign fabric nothing
# tells the proxy that a link died (doc/audit/2026-09-24_p4-driven-api-shape/ANALYSIS.md
# section 5). `heartbeat start` runs a small root daemon that sends one frame per period out of
# every switch-side veth with a switch at its far end, reads it back at that far end, and writes
# down WHEN each direction was last heard. `tc netem` sits on the same egress qdisc the frame
# goes through, so a cut link cuts the heartbeat with it.
#
# 🔴 WHAT ROOT WILL NOT DO HERE, each with a test in tests/shell/test_ndtwin_lab_heartbeat.sh
#    that has been seen red (tests/shell/mutate_ndtwin_lab_heartbeat.sh):
#
#   * take ANYTHING from the caller but the sub-verb. `heartbeat start|stop|status` and nothing
#     after it -- no interface, no path, no ethertype, no period. The daemon's own argv is the
#     single word `daemon`, and its program refuses any other shape too.
#   * choose interfaces itself. They come from the running fabric's own declaration, the switch
#     manifest the ROOT topology process writes (/tmp/ndtwin_p4_switches.json), believed only
#     when it is a root-owned, not group/other-writable regular file -- read with O_NOFOLLOW |
#     O_NONBLOCK, the rule tools/p4_power_helper.py already applies to the same file. up.target
#     and the package's topology.json were the other candidates and both are adam-writable
#     descriptions of what was ASKED FOR; this is the record of what RUNS. Every interface must
#     be `<switch>-eth<port>` as bmv2 was launched with it, be a veth, and a link is two of them
#     whose kernel iflinks name each other. Everything else is listened on and never sent on.
#   * start without a fabric: no topo session, no manifest, or a manifest whose switches are not
#     all live bmv2 processes -- refused before anything is launched.
#   * run twice. A live daemon (pidfile + cmdline + uid) makes `start` say so and launch nothing;
#     two racing `start`s are settled by an exclusive flock the daemon takes before it does
#     anything else.
#   * signal a stranger. `stop` signals the pid in the pidfile only after /proc/<pid>/cmdline is
#     EXACTLY the daemon's argv and /proc/<pid>/status says uid 0 -- never a pattern, never
#     pkill/pgrep (G-9, above).
#   * decide. The daemon reports "direction X last heard at t"; whether that is DOWN, and what to
#     reroute, is the proxy's call with the proxy's constants (segment W).
#
# 🔑 THE PROGRAM IS INSIDE THIS FILE, not in KERNEL_DIR, and that is the point of it: this file is
#    the root-owned, sha-pinned copy `ndt status` compares (FINDING #83), so what root runs for
#    the heartbeat is exactly what was reviewed and installed. A daemon that lived in the
#    adam-writable tree would make every check above a suggestion. It is written to
#    $HB_RUN_DIR (root-only) at each start and executed from there, which is also what gives the
#    process an argv `stop` can recognise.
#
# Where the report goes, and why a file: $HB_RUN_DIR/heartbeat.json, 0644 in a 0755 root directory
# -- the proxy reads it (segment W). The other design, the daemon POSTing to the proxy, would put
# a root process in the business of finding and trusting an adam-owned HTTP service, and would
# open a route anything on localhost could post fake link states to.

